{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FtvRYU2rGSjMRjqQ5y72on","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the key difference. In `cat/main.go` at line 43, there's an explicit check:\n\n```go\nif errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE) {\n    os.Exit(0)\n}\n```\n\nThe `cat` implementation checks for EPIPE errors and explicitly exits 0 when it gets one. The `uniq` implementation doesn't have this check — it just treats all write errors as exit code 1.\n\nThe issue is a race between the SIGPIPE signal handler goroutine and the error return path. When the write fails with EPIPE:\n\n\n- The kernel sends SIGPIPE\n- The write call returns an EPIPE error\n- There's a race: will the signal handler goroutine run first (exit 0), or will the error propagation path run first (exit 1)?\n\nCat handles this by explicitly checking for EPIPE in the error path. Uniq needs the same treatment. Looking at the uniq code flow, `deduplicate()` calls `emitRun()` which does writes, and if that fails, it returns an error that propagates up as exit code 1. The same issue happens with `w.Flush()` at the end — any write error gets treated as a fatal failure instead of checking whether it's specifically an EPIPE that should exit cleanly.\n\nI need to add EPIPE checks at the three places where write errors cause exit 1: in `deduplicate` when `emitRun()` fails, in `deduplicate` when `w.Flush()` fails, and in `main` when `w.Flush()` fails. The fix is to check if the error is EPIPE and exit 0 instead of 1 in those cases.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2588,"cache_read_input_tokens":41927,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2588},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3cde0789-9cb